Fix black/undersized mouse cursor when running Rose Tattoo in hires mode.

Root cause: when SCUMMVM_SHERLOCK_TATTOO_HIRES_SCALE > 1 and the hires
format is not CLUT8 (rgb565/rgba32), Sherlock::Screen::setPalette()
early-returned WITHOUT forwarding the palette to the backend at all,
to avoid tripping the pixel-format assert in the base
Graphics::Screen::setPalette() (the hires composite surface itself is
no longer 1 byte/pixel, so the base class's assert(bytesPerPixel == 1)
would fail).

However, ScummVM's mouse cursor (loaded from rmouse.vgs) stays an 8-bit
palettized image regardless of the hires screen format, and cursor
rendering needs a color palette to make sense of it. Since the game
palette was never being forwarded in hires-noCLUT8 mode, the cursor's
color mapping stayed at its zero-initialized state and the cursor
rendered solid black.

First attempt: call g_system->getPaletteManager()->setPalette()
directly (bypassing the Graphics::Screen pixel-format assert). This
compiled, but crashed at runtime on the OpenGL backend:

  Assertion failed: (_gameScreen->hasPalette()), function setPalette,
  file opengl-graphics.cpp, line 1448.

OpenGLGraphicsManager::setPalette() also asserts the *game* screen
texture itself is palettized - not just an assert in the generic
Graphics::Screen wrapper - so forwarding to the game palette manager
is unsafe once the hires composite has switched to RGB565/RGBA32.

Final fix: use CursorMan.replaceCursorPalette() instead. This is
ScummVM's dedicated *cursor* palette mechanism (backed by
OSystem::setCursorPalette() / OSystem::kFeatureCursorPalette), which
is completely independent of the game screen's own pixel format - both
the SDL and OpenGL backends support it unconditionally, so it doesn't
require the game screen to be CLUT8. This correctly colors the 8-bit
palettized cursor without touching the (now RGB565/RGBA32) main game
screen palette path at all.

Additionally, Events::setCursor() only special-cased upscaling the
cursor bitmap for the 3DO engine's `_isScreenDoubled` 2x path; for PC
(Rose Tattoo) it always passed CursorMan.replaceCursor() the native,
unscaled cursor bitmap, leaving the cursor visually tiny relative to
the enlarged hires window. Generalized the same nearest-neighbor
upscaling approach to the PC 8-bit path using roseTattooHiresScale().

--- a/engines/sherlock/screen.cpp
+++ b/engines/sherlock/screen.cpp
@@ -31,6 +31,7 @@
 #include "common/system.h"
 #include "common/util.h"
 #include "graphics/pixelformat.h"
 #include "graphics/palette.h"
+#include "graphics/cursorman.h"
 #include "image/png.h"

@@ -172,8 +173,20 @@ void Screen::setPalette(const byte *palette, uint start, uint num) {
 	assert(start + num <= Graphics::PALETTE_COUNT);
 	Common::copy(palette, palette + num * 3, &_roseTattooPalette[start * 3]);

-	if (_roseTattooHiresScale > 1 && !_roseTattooHiresFormat.isCLUT8())
+	if (_roseTattooHiresScale > 1 && !_roseTattooHiresFormat.isCLUT8()) {
+		// Graphics::Screen::setPalette() forwards to
+		// g_system->getPaletteManager()->setPalette(), which some backends
+		// (e.g. OpenGLGraphicsManager::setPalette()) assert requires the
+		// game screen itself to be palettized/CLUT8 - no longer true once
+		// we've switched the backend to RGB565/RGBA32 for the hires
+		// composite, so we can't call it here without crashing.
+		//
+		// However, the mouse cursor (an 8-bit palettized image) still needs
+		// a palette to be rendered in color: use CursorMan's dedicated
+		// cursor-palette mechanism (backed by OSystem::setCursorPalette(),
+		// independent of the game screen's own pixel format) instead of the
+		// regular game palette, so the cursor doesn't render solid black.
+		CursorMan.replaceCursorPalette(palette, start, num);
 		return;
+	}

 	Graphics::Screen::setPalette(palette, start, num);
 }

